From 1fe5e74d839459f0dbb5d24129892a0b3dc58526 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Wed, 2 Sep 2026 11:04:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 10769/11362] Make the planner keep its two output files
 consistent (REQ-RESEARCH-6890)

The premise I was given -- an id-range reservation bug -- does not exist. There is
no allocator reserving ranges. One planner run creates BOTH
openspec/change-proposals/research-roadmap-vNEXT.md and research-roadmap-next.yaml,
and it wrote a document declaring "13 tasks, exp6885 through exp6897" alongside a
YAML holding 4. The day before it claimed 11 tasks for .602 alongside a YAML holding
4. The planner is inconsistent with itself; nothing downstream trims the list.

Confirmed rather than assumed: the .603 document and the .603 roadmap were written
by the same planner run at 13:29 UTC, which logged "4 tasks proposed".

Each milestone's own contract experiment caught it -- exp6874 on
v602_document_yaml_parity, exp6885 on document_yaml_task_contract -- but only AFTER
activation, and in .602 the root failure cascade-blocked exp6875 through exp6877, so
3 of 4 real tasks never ran.

The fix is a prompt requirement, deliberately not an activation gate. A guard that
refuses on mismatch would deadlock the loop if the planner reproduced the mismatch
on replan, and the conductor allows only two replans. The per-task contract check
already detects the condition; this aims to stop it being produced. The instruction
carries its incident, because a rule without its cost gets edited away by someone
who does not know it.

6 tests, 3 of 3 mutations RED. One asserts placement between FILE 2 and the optional
fields section, since the instruction is useless 40 lines away from the file list,
and one scans the new fragment for unescaped braces, which would break the planner
at format time rather than in review.

Takes effect on the conductor's next restart: it runs as a long-lived --loop
process and will not re-read this file before then.

